# Needs review

Things carried across the migration deliberately, that somebody should look at once the site is live. Nothing here is broken; all of it is inherited from the Webflow site as it stands.

## An n8n **test** webhook is live on every tour page

All 29 `/tour/*` pages post to:

```
https://sygnal-n8n-u1282.vm.elestio.app/webhook-test/5be21ed5-f753-4492-89d3-4d7e1206b9ca
```

**Carried across unchanged**, on instruction, because changing it would alter live behaviour during a migration whose whole point is that nothing changes.

Two things make it worth a look:

-   `/webhook-test/` is n8n's **test** path, not `/webhook/`. In n8n a test URL only responds while the workflow is open in the editor with "Listen for test event" active — a production workflow answers on `/webhook/<id>`. So either this has been silently failing on the live site, or the workflow is being left in test mode on purpose.

-   It is a Sygnal-hosted endpoint on an Elestio VM, so it is ours to check.

Worth confirming what the workflow is for before the Webflow account is cancelled, since that is the point at which there is no original left to compare against.

## `/search` is gone, by decision

Webflow's built-in site search backed `/search`, and it has no equivalent after cut-over. The page is not ported and the URL is not redirected, so it now answers 404.

The search FORM is still in the shared nav on every page — it is part of the frozen Webflow markup — and it posts to `/search`. Decide whether to remove the field from the nav or point it somewhere, because as it stands the box is visible and submitting it lands on a 404.

## `/sitemap` links five specials at a URL that does not exist

The human-readable `/sitemap` page lists the five specials and links each one at
`/special/<slug>`. **That URL 404s on the live site today** — there is no `/special/` route and
there never was one in the export. The rows are reproduced exactly as found, links included,
because inventing a destination is not a migration decision.

Two ways out, whenever you want one: point them at `/tours` the way the dead redirects now are, or
drop the list. Neither is urgent — it is exactly as broken as it has been.

## `/sitemap`'s area rows link nowhere

The same page lists all fifteen areas with `href="#"`. The designer never bound a link to that row
in Webflow, so clicking an area does nothing. Reproduced as found. `/map` lists the same fifteen
areas with working `/area/<slug>` links, so the information is reachable — the sitemap's copy just
is not clickable.

## Eight of Webflow's redirects pointed at 404s

Webflow's `manifest.json` declared 19 redirects. Eight of them targeted tour URLs that no longer resolve — `/cosplay`, `/gallery/cosplay`, `/matakana`, `/waipoua`, `/tour/gs16anr-gs48s` and three `/tours/tour?id=…` entries. They were dropped rather than carried, on instruction.

> Redirect these all to /tours

**Done.** All of them now 301 to `/tours`. The three `/tours/tour?id=…` are covered by a single
`/tours/tour` entry, because `_redirects` matches on path and the id cannot be matched — pointing
the path at the index rather than guessing which tour the visitor wanted.

## The sitemap had an entry that 404s

`/tour/hobbiton-rotorua-tour-kuirau-park-footbath-experience` was listed in the live sitemap and answers 404 on the live site. Dropped from the new sitemap, on instruction.

> Redirect to /tours

**Done.** It 301s to `/tours`, and stays out of the sitemap — a 301'd URL must not be listed, or
the canonical becomes ambiguous.

## Google Fonts stays external

Type is loaded from `fonts.googleapis.com` and `fonts.gstatic.com` via `webfont.js` on `ajax.googleapis.com`. Not Webflow, so it survives the cut-over untouched — but it is a third-party dependency on the critical render path and a privacy consideration. Self-hosting is a separate, deliberate job.

> Stays external

## The Webflow JS runtime ships as-is

jQuery plus Webflow's webpack chunks, now served locally from `/wf/js/`. It drives the nav dropdowns and every IX2 scroll animation, including `opacity: 0` elements that IX2 animates to 1 — so the runtime is load-bearing, not decorative. Dropping it would make those elements invisible rather than merely unanimated. Replacing it is a redesign, not a migration.
